{{Life in Balisca}}

'''The Cosmopolitan Church (of Cannabis)''', often shortened to the '''Cosmopolitan Church''', is a decentralized [[W:nontheistic religion|nontheistic religious]] {{W|organization}} that is primarily based in the [[Yerba Buena]], [[Balisca]], with additional congregations in [[California]], and [[Sonora]]. Co-founded by [[Jeroni Baliarda]], the organization's spokesperson, and Jèssica Canyelles on 20 April 2001, the organization uses {{W|cannabis}} as a sacrament to promote {{W|egalitarianism}}, {{W|peace}}ful [[W:Coexistence|coexistence]], respect of {{W|human rights}}, and the {{W|separation of church and state}}, supporting their mission "to encourage [[W:Secular spirituality|benevolence and empathy]] [among all people]." The Cosmopolitan Church has utilized {{W|Internet memes}}, [[W:religious satire|satire]], theatrical ploys, humor, and legal action in their public campaigns to "generate attention and prompt people to reevaluate fears and perceptions", and to "highlight societal hypocrisy". It gained government recognition in 2008, and it became the largest religious organization in Balisca in 2020, at the beginning of the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}}.

Membership is open to anyone who is willing to hear the Church's message of peace, even those who are adherents to {{W|Theism|theistic religion}}s. The process of acceptance into the Cosmopolitan Church, called "{{W|Substance intoxication|elevation}}" is through a "{{W|Cannabis culture|smoke sesh}}" involving the local church members, who through smoking {{W|cannabis (drug)|cannabis}} (called the "holy herb") together embark on a "spiritual journey" in which people converse, discussing a variety of topics such as their background, their ideas of {{W|meaning of life}}, and their dreams and goals. It's founders claim this a {{W|relaxation technique}} designed to break down barriers, reduce anxiety, and to promote acceptance and understanding between people of different backgrounds.

The organization's participation in public affairs includes political and {{W|lobbying}} efforts outside of Balisca, with a focus on exposing and combating the discrimination tied to {{W|Theism|theistic religions}}, and exposing {{W|human rights abuses}}. Because the group also regards {{W|bodily integrity|inviolability of the body}} as a key doctrine, it also views {{W|drug prohibition}}, {{W|circumcision}}, all [[W:Abortion-rights movements|restrictions on abortion]] (including mandatory waiting periods), as an infringement on the rights of its members to practice their religion.
